.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt VHOST-NET 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vhost-net
.Nd in-kernel virtio network backend for a tap interface
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/vhost-net
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The character device
.Pa /dev/vhost-net ,
10:238 as on Linux, moves the frames of a virtio network device of a
virtual machine between the guest's virtqueues and a
.Xr tap 4
interface inside the kernel, so that the program that runs the machine
reads and writes none of them.
QEMU's
.Ql -netdev tap,...,vhost=on
is its user.
Each open is a device of its own.
.Pp
The owner, the process that calls
.Dv VHOST_SET_OWNER ,
gives the device the guest's memory as a table of regions, each a
guest-physical range and the address of the owner's mapping of it; the
size, base and addresses of the receive ring, ring 0, and the transmit
ring, ring 1; for each ring an eventfd the guest's doorbell signals, the
kick, which QEMU makes a KVM ioeventfd, an eventfd the device signals to
raise the ring's interrupt, the call, which QEMU makes a KVM irqfd, and an
eventfd signalled when the guest gives a bad descriptor; and last the tap
descriptor, with
.Dv VHOST_NET_SET_BACKEND .
The device pins the regions' pages, which count against the owner's
.Dv RLIMIT_MEMLOCK .
.Pp
A frame the stack sends on the tap goes into the next buffer the guest
offers on its receive ring at once, in the sender's context, after a
virtio header of no offload with num_buffers 1; a frame that does not fit
the buffer is dropped and the buffer stays the guest's, and a frame that
finds no buffer waits in the tap, which holds 1024, until the guest's next
kick.
A frame the guest puts on its transmit ring is taken by the kernel thread
.Li vhost-net ,
which the kick wakes, and given to the stack as a
.Xr write 2
of the tap would give it, its virtio header stripped; a pass takes at
most 128 frames before the thread gives way.
The device asks the guest for no kicks while it can go on without them,
and raises a ring's interrupt unless the guest asks for none.
.Ss Requests
A request's argument is a pointer, as in
.In linux/vhost.h .
A ring's state is a
.Vt struct vhost_vring_state ,
its index and a number; a ring's file a
.Vt struct vhost_vring_file ,
its index and a descriptor, \-1 for none.
.Bl -tag -width VHOST_SET_BACKEND_FEATURES
.It Dv VHOST_SET_OWNER
Makes the caller's address space the owner.
Every request below but the features' then comes from it.
.It Dv VHOST_RESET_OWNER
Stops both rings and lets go of the tap, the eventfds, the pins and the
owner.
.It Dv VHOST_GET_FEATURES
Writes the features the device offers:
.Dv VHOST_NET_F_VIRTIO_NET_HDR ,
bit 27, the virtio header added and stripped here, which the guest sees as
.Dv VIRTIO_F_ANY_LAYOUT ;
.Dv VIRTIO_F_NOTIFY_ON_EMPTY ,
bit 24; and
.Dv VIRTIO_F_VERSION_1 ,
bit 32.
.It Dv VHOST_SET_FEATURES
Takes the features negotiated.
With
.Dv VHOST_NET_F_VIRTIO_NET_HDR
the header is 12 bytes with
.Dv VIRTIO_F_VERSION_1
and 10 without; without it the frames carry none.
.It Dv VHOST_GET_BACKEND_FEATURES , VHOST_SET_BACKEND_FEATURES
None are offered, and only 0 is taken.
.It Dv VHOST_SET_MEM_TABLE
Takes a
.Vt struct vhost_memory
of up to 64 regions and pins each, keeping the pin of a region the old
table has whole.
A region that cannot be pinned, such as device memory, is kept without a
pin, and a ring or buffer in it stops its ring.
.It Dv VHOST_SET_VRING_NUM
The ring's size, a power of two up to 32768.
.It Dv VHOST_SET_VRING_BASE , VHOST_GET_VRING_BASE
The next entry of the available ring the device takes.
.It Dv VHOST_SET_VRING_ADDR
Takes a
.Vt struct vhost_vring_addr :
the descriptor table on 16 bytes, the used ring on 4 and the available
ring on 2, at the owner's addresses.
.It Dv VHOST_SET_VRING_KICK , VHOST_SET_VRING_CALL , VHOST_SET_VRING_ERR
The ring's eventfds.
.It Dv VHOST_NET_SET_BACKEND
Gives the ring the interface of the tap descriptor, or takes it away
with \-1; the tap is let go once neither ring has it.
.El
.Pp
When the guest's memory is unmapped, both rings stop until a new table.
When the tap's last descriptor closes, the rings lose it.
The last close of the device's descriptor stops it and lets everything go.
.Sh LIMITS
The split ring alone: no indirect descriptors, no event index
.Pq Dv VIRTIO_RING_F_EVENT_IDX ,
no mergeable receive buffers
.Pq Dv VIRTIO_NET_F_MRG_RXBUF ,
no offload, no dirty log for migration, no IOTLB and one queue pair.
Both rings take the same tap, where Linux lets each have its own.
One kernel thread serves every device, where Linux has one for each.
A region of the table is pinned, where Linux reaches it through the
owner's mappings, so the guest's memory counts against
.Dv RLIMIT_MEMLOCK .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/vhost-net
.It Pa /dev/vhost-net
The device, read and written by root.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Give a QEMU guest the interface
.Li tap0
with its frames moved in the kernel:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# qemu-system-x86_64 -enable-kvm \e
    -netdev tap,id=n0,ifname=tap0,script=no,downscript=no,vhost=on \e
    -device virtio-net-pci,netdev=n0 ...
# ifconfig tap0 192.168.100.1 netmask 255.255.255.0 up
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Er E2BIG
A table of more than 64 regions.
.It Er EBADF
A ring's file names no descriptor.
.It Er EBUSY
A second
.Dv VHOST_SET_OWNER ;
a ring's size or base changed while it has the tap; a tap another device
has, or one other than the other ring's.
.It Er EFAULT
An argument is not the caller's; a ring given the tap has no size or
address, or lies outside the pinned memory.
.It Er EINVAL
A ring size that is no power of two up to 32768, a base past 65535, a
ring not aligned, or a ring's file that is no eventfd.
.It Er ENOBUFS
A ring index past 1.
.It Er ENOMEM
No memory, or a pin past
.Dv RLIMIT_MEMLOCK ;
the old table stays.
.It Er ENOTSOCK
.Dv VHOST_NET_SET_BACKEND
of a descriptor that is no tap with an interface.
.It Er ENOTTY
An unknown request.
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
A feature or backend feature not offered, or a ring address flag other
than the log's.
.It Er EPERM
A request but the features' before
.Dv VHOST_SET_OWNER ,
or from a process whose address space is not the owner's.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr eventfd 2 ,
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr kvm 4 ,
.Xr tap 4 ,
.Xr virtio-net 4
.Sh HISTORY
The device follows Linux's drivers/vhost/net.c and drivers/vhost/vhost.c
and the numbers of linux/vhost.h.
